        "payload": "tree 53379fcac8c3441f8903ae094f0e8f729de66288\nparent 649bc0a645eb127a9b1e6d078f8d129be6380c87\nauthor Ferdinando Ametrano <ferdinando@ametrano.net> 1792037043 +0200\ncommitter Ferdinando Ametrano <ferdinando@ametrano.net> 1792038585 +0200\n\nbuild: pass hardening flags to secp256k1\n\nThe hardening flags are added to core_interface, which the secp256k1\nsubtree does not use: cmake/secp256k1.cmake passes it only the\nsanitizer and APPEND_* flags. Its objects are therefore built without\n_FORTIFY_SOURCE, -fstack-protector-all, -fstack-clash-protection,\n-fcf-protection and -mbranch-protection, unless the compiler enables\nthem by default.\n\nThis also drops the control flow property from the binaries. The\nlinker marks an executable as BTI/PAC (aarch64) or IBT/SHSTK (x86_64)\nenabled only when every object it links has the property. On aarch64\nUbuntu 26.04 with GCC 15, bitcoind built from master has no AArch64\nfeature property, and the secp256k1 objects are the only objects in\nthe build tree without it.\n\nMove the hardening flags to a hardening_interface library, which\ncore_interface links, and pass its compile and link options to the\nsubtree in the same way as sanitize_interface. This brings back the\nhardening_interface that was folded into core_interface in 77e553ab6a,\nnow that the flags have a second user.\n",
